; ======================================================================
;  hw_gpio_q.pi4 - the Arduino UNO Q HwGpio* backend. THE BOARD SIDE OF
;  THE HwGpio* SEAM declared in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi.
;
;  Anvil/Core/gpio_cmd.pbi (the `gpio` command) calls these names and
;  never touches a register, a mux value or a raw pull code; this file is
;  thin wrappers over ArduinoQ/Lib/tlmm.unoq, whose header carries all the
;  register-level reasoning - the $00500000 base, the 4 KB per-pin
;  stride, the 4-bit mux field, the keeper-that-is-not-a-pull-up, and the
;  eGPIO ownership bit.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER, and it is not arbitrary:
;    * ArduinoQ/Lib/tlmm.unoq  BEFORE this file - it defines TlmmModeGet,
;      TlmmRead, TlmmPullGet, TlmmFuncGet and the #TLMM_* raw codes this
;      file translates. A library never includes a library (house rule
;      10), so board.pi4 lists both, tlmm.pi4 first.
;    * Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi      BEFORE this file - the #HW_* vocabulary
;      these wrappers return.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT CHANGED, AND WHY THE OLD ANSWER WAS WRONG
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  board.pi4 used to say, and qstubs_q.pi4 used to implement:
;
;      #CAP_GPIO = 0   ; NOT REACHABLE. The UNO Q's header pins are owned
;                      ; by the STM32U585 co-processor, and the QCM2290's
;                      ; own TLMM is not driven. Anvil has no pins here
;                      ; at all.
;
;  The first half of that is TRUE and survives - see NO USER HEADER
;  below. The second half was the error, and it is the same shape of
;  error the console blocker was: a true fact about one path was allowed
;  to stand for a conclusion about all of them. The QCM2290 has 127 TLMM
;  lines of its own at $00500000, thirty of which are demonstrably in use
;  by on-board functions, and they are reachable by plain MMIO from a
;  UEFI application at EL1. "Anvil has no pins here at all" was false.
;
;  MEASURED, not assumed - the same discipline the ConOut/GOP question
;  got. Anvil's own `memory` command reads arbitrary addresses, so the
;  TLMM was read on the running board with the image that was already
;  deployed, before a line of this file was allowed to run.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  NO USER HEADER - HwGpioUserMax() RETURNS -1, AND THAT IS A FINDING
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ***THE ARDUINO HEADERS ON THIS BOARD DO NOT REACH THE QCM2290.*** They
;  are wired to the STM32U585 co-processor, which Anvil does not drive.
;  So there is no "user header" in the sense hal.pi4 means - no range of
;  TLMM pins an operator can put a jumper on - and this backend says so
;  by returning -1 rather than inventing a plausible-looking range.
;
;  THIS IS NOT AN INFERENCE. It was traced pin by pin through the
;  schematic (UNO-Q-ABX00162-schematics.pdf sheet 20, "IO"), and every
;  one of the thirty-two pins on JDIGITAL1 and JANALOG1 lands on an
;  STM32U585 ball - D13 is the MCU's SPI2 clock on PB13, A0 is PA4, D0
;  and D1 are the MCU's USART1, and even IOREF and RESET are the MCU's
;  3.3 V rail and its NRST. There is no mux, no buffer and no alternate
;  path: the only parts in the way are 0-ohm series resistors. The
;  datasheet says the same in section 9.6/9.7, and the Qwiic connector is
;  on the MCU's I2C4 as well.
;
;  So a bare-metal TLMM driver on this SoC cannot toggle a single Arduino
;  header pin, and no amount of work on this file changes that. Reaching
;  D0..D13 means going THROUGH the STM32 - over the SPI bridge on serial
;  engine 5 or the LPUART bridge on serial engine 2 - which is a
;  different piece of work with a protocol at the far end.
;
;  WHAT THE SoC DOES EXPOSE, for whoever wants a pin to wave at: the
;  60-pin JMISC1 mezzanine carries twelve TLMM lines at 1.8 V - GPIO 0,
;  1, 2, 3, 82 and 86 on its odd pins 37..47, GPIO 18 and 28 on 49 and
;  51, and GPIO 98..101 on 46..52 (schematic sheet 9 for the ball
;  assignments, sheet 20 for the connector). Of those, 0 and 1 are in use
;  as I2C bus 0 and are reserved below; 98..101 are eGPIO pins whose
;  ownership must be claimed before they can be driven, which
;  TlmmModeSet() does. That mezzanine, not the Arduino header, is where a
;  jumper would go on this board.
;
;  That -1 is not a gap in this file; it is the honest answer, and the
;  core already knows what to do with it. Anvil/Core/gpio_cmd.pbi:159-166
;  prints "This board has general-purpose I/O but no user header this
;  command knows about ... Type gpio all to see every line" instead of
;  silently reporting an empty range. A board that returned, say, 27 here
;  to make `gpio` look more like the Pi 4 would be inviting an operator
;  to drive pins that are not where they think they are.
;
;  Every one of the 127 lines is still READABLE and reportable through
;  `gpio all`, and the free ones are still drivable by number. What does
;  not exist is a header to call them.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHERE THE RESERVED LIST COMES FROM
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  From TWO sources that know different things, and the union of them,
;  because taking either one alone leaves a hole big enough to stop the
;  board booting.
;
;    1. THE RUNNING BOARD'S OWN DEVICE TREE, extracted over SSH from
;       /proc/device-tree while Debian was up and saved as
;       ArduinoQ/Reference/unoq-live-device-tree.dts (model "Arduino
;       SA,Imola"). That file describes THIS board, which qrb2210-rb1.dts
;       - the Qualcomm reference board - does not: the RB1 has an LT9611
;       HDMI bridge where the UNO Q has an ANX7625 on USB-C, so taking
;       the RB1's pin usage would have reserved the wrong pins.
;    2. THE SCHEMATIC, UNO-Q-ABX00162-schematics.pdf, sheet 9
;       ("QRB2210-GPIO") for the SoC ball-to-net map, sheet 20 ("IO") for
;       the connectors and sheet 24 for the display bridge, plus the
;       datasheet's section 9.5.
;
;  ***WHY BOTH, AND WHAT USING ONLY THE FIRST COST.*** The device tree is
;  authoritative about what LINUX owns, and completely silent about what
;  the SoC reads at RESET. Boot straps are sampled by the boot ROM before
;  an operating system exists, so no driver claims them and no node
;  mentions them. Built from the device tree alone, this list declared
;  GPIO 48, 50, 51, 53, 61 and 62 FREE TO DRIVE - and the schematic shows
;  the first four are the BOOT_CONFIG straps that decide where the SoC
;  boots from and the last two are the board-identity straps. Those are
;  in Tier 0 below now.
;
;  The device tree knows the drivers. The schematic knows the copper.
;  Neither alone is the list.
;
;  Every entry below cites the line in that file that owns the pin. The
;  list is deliberately WIDER than "pins some driver has claimed", and
;  the tiers say why each pin is in it, because the cost of the two kinds
;  of mistake is not symmetric: refusing a pin that was actually free
;  costs an operator one command, and allowing a pin that carries the
;  console costs the whole session with no way to see what happened.
;
;  TIER A - actively owned by an enabled node in the live device tree.
;  TIER B - named for the STM32U585 bridge by an orphaned pinctrl state:
;           the state exists and names the pin, but nothing in the tree
;           references it, because THE STM32 IS NOT IN THE DEVICE TREE AT
;           ALL (zero hits for "stm32" in 3487 lines - the bridge is
;           managed outside the kernel). The names are still evidence,
;           and mcu_nrst on GPIO 38 is a pin that resets the co-processor
;           if it is driven. Reserved on the strength of the names, with
;           the uncertainty stated rather than hidden.
;  TIER C - the alternate-function partners of buses that are enabled on
;           their OTHER pins, or pins belonging to a disabled controller.
;           Free by the letter of the device tree; likely to have board
;           traces on them. Reserved, and said to be advisory.
;
;  READING ANY PIN STAYS ALLOWED - this gates WRITES only, exactly as the
;  seam specifies. The status report deliberately shows these pins,
;  because that is how you see that the console is on a qup function and
;  the display bridge's interrupt is pulled up. What is refused is
;  RE-configuring them out from under the running machine.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Extent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioCount()
  ; Every TLMM line the QCM2290 has - 127, numbered 0..126.
  ; agatti.dtsi:473 `gpio-ranges = <&tlmm 0 0 127>`, and independently
  ; pinctrl-qcm2290.c:1089 `.ngpios = 127`. The live board's own device
  ; tree says it a third time (unoq-live-device-tree.dts:1414).
  ProcedureReturn #TLMM_PIN_COUNT
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioUserMax()
  ; -1 = this board has no user header the QCM2290 can see. See NO USER
  ; HEADER above; this is a measured fact about the board, not a gap.
  ProcedureReturn -1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Mode - normalised to the #HW_GPIO_* vocabulary.
;
;  TlmmModeGet already asks the two questions in the right order (is the
;  mux non-zero, and only then what does OE say), so this is a plain
;  mapping. It is written out rather than passed through for the reason
;  hw_gpio.pi4 gives on the Pi 4: the two enumerations live in different
;  files and a future divergence must fail here, visibly.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioModeGet(pin.i)
  Define m.i
  m = TlmmModeGet(pin)
  If m < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If m = #TLMM_INPUT
    ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_IN
  EndIf
  If m = #TLMM_OUTPUT
    ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_OUT
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn #HW_GPIO_ALT
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwGpioAltGet - the alternate-function NUMBER for an ALT pin, or -1 for
;  a bad pin or a plain input/output.
;
;  ON THIS PART THE ALT NUMBER IS THE MUX FIELD, UNCHANGED. There is no
;  table to undo here, and that is worth saying because the Pi 4's
;  counterpart is nothing but such a table - the BCM2711's ALT4 and ALT5
;  are the raw codes 3 and 2, so its HwGpioAltGet is a six-way Select
;  that puts them back in order. The QCM2290 has no such quirk: the mux
;  field holds the function INDEX directly (pinctrl-msm.c:237 writes the
;  index, `val |= i << g->mux_bit`), so 1 means the pin's first alternate
;  function, 2 its second, and so on up to 15.
;
;  WHAT THAT NUMBER MEANS IS PER-PIN, and this backend does not pretend
;  otherwise. Each pin has its own function list in pinctrl-qcm2290.c, so
;  function 1 is "qup0" on GPIO 0 and something else entirely on GPIO 40.
;  Naming them would mean carrying a 127 x 10 table of Qualcomm function
;  names into a board file; the number is what a reader checks against
;  the device tree's pinmux nodes anyway, and it is what the seam asks
;  for.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioAltGet(pin.i)
  Define f.i
  f = TlmmFuncGet(pin)
  If f < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If f = #TLMM_FUNC_GPIO
    ProcedureReturn -1         ; a plain GPIO is not an alternate function
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn f
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Level - the actual pad, read back, which is what the `gpio` command
;  promises its reader. TlmmRead returns 0/1/-1 already.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioLevelGet(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn TlmmRead(pin)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pull.
;
;  ***THE KEEPER IS REPORTED AS "NO PULL", DELIBERATELY.*** The QCM2290's
;  two-bit pull field has four states and hal.pi4's portable vocabulary
;  has three: the silicon's value 2 is a BUS KEEPER (a weak latch that
;  holds the last level) and there is no #HW_PULL_KEEPER to map it to.
;  Of the three available answers, "none" is the only one that is not a
;  lie - a keeper is not a resistor to a rail, so calling it a pull-up or
;  a pull-down would misstate the electrical condition of the pin to
;  someone deciding whether they need an external resistor.
;
;  Losing information at a seam is acceptable; inventing it is not. The
;  raw four-state value stays available from TlmmPullGet for anything
;  that wants it, and the day the portable vocabulary grows a keeper this
;  is the one line that changes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioPullGet(pin.i)
  Define p.i
  p = TlmmPullGet(pin)
  If p < 0
    ProcedureReturn -1
  EndIf
  If p = #TLMM_PULL_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_NONE
  EndIf
  If p = #TLMM_PULL_DOWN
    ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_DOWN
  EndIf
  If p = #TLMM_PULL_UP
    ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_UP
  EndIf
  ; #TLMM_KEEPER - see above.
  ProcedureReturn #HW_PULL_NONE
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The write seam.
;
;  HwGpioMode takes a #HW_GPIO_* code. #HW_GPIO_ALT is refused (returns
;  0): "make this pin some alternate function" is not a whole
;  instruction - WHICH alternate is, and that is TlmmFuncSet's job, which
;  a later HwGpioAlt(pin, n) will expose. Refusing the ambiguous case
;  rather than guessing a function is the same discipline the Pi 4
;  backend uses.
;
;  These are NOT gated by the reserved list. The gating happens one layer
;  up: Anvil/Core/gpio_cmd.pbi calls HwGpioReservedReason first and
;  refuses before it reaches these, which is what the seam specifies.
;  Putting the check in both places would mean two lists to keep in step.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioMode(pin.i, mode.i)
  If mode = #HW_GPIO_IN
    ProcedureReturn TlmmModeSet(pin, #TLMM_INPUT)
  EndIf
  If mode = #HW_GPIO_OUT
    ProcedureReturn TlmmModeSet(pin, #TLMM_OUTPUT)
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioWrite(pin.i, level.i)
  ProcedureReturn TlmmWrite(pin, level)
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwGpioToggle(pin.i)
  ProcedureReturn TlmmToggle(pin)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwGpioReservedReason - the pins the BOARD ITSELF is using, that the
;  `gpio` write subcommands must refuse to reconfigure.
;
;  Returns 0 for a pin free to drive, or the address of a lowercase
;  fragment naming the function that owns it. The fragment reads after
;  "GPIO n is " and the core prints it with UartWriteStr, exactly as
;  RequireCap prints its *reason - so the core never learns which pins
;  these are.
;
;  Ordered most-dangerous first, so a reader meets the console before
;  anything else. See WHERE THE RESERVED LIST COMES FROM for the tiers
;  and the citations.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwGpioReservedReason(pin.i)

  ; ---- TIER 0: STRAPS AND POWER. THE ONES THE DEVICE TREE CANNOT TELL
  ;      YOU ABOUT, AND THE ONES THAT DO NOT MERELY BREAK A DRIVER -----
  ;
  ;  ***THESE WERE MISSING FROM THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS LIST, AND THAT
  ;  IS THE WHOLE LESSON OF THIS SECTION.*** The first list was built from
  ;  the running board's device tree, which is authoritative about what
  ;  LINUX owns and says NOTHING about what the SoC reads at reset. Boot
  ;  straps are sampled by the boot ROM before any operating system
  ;  exists, so no driver ever claims them and no device tree ever
  ;  mentions them - they looked FREE, and six of them were in the list of
  ;  pins this backend was about to declare safe to drive.
  ;
  ;  They come from the SCHEMATIC instead (UNO-Q-ABX00162-schematics.pdf,
  ;  sheet 9 "QRB2210-GPIO", with the connector side on sheet 20 and the
  ;  display bridge on sheet 24) and from the datasheet's section 9.5.
  ;  Two sources that disagree about which pins matter, and the union is
  ;  the only safe answer: the device tree knows the drivers, the
  ;  schematic knows the copper.
  If pin = 48 Or pin = 50 Or pin = 51 Or pin = 53
    ProcedureReturn "one of the four BOOT_CONFIG straps the boot ROM samples at reset to decide where the SoC boots from: no operating system ever claims these, so nothing in the device tree warns you, and driving one can stop the board booting at all"
  EndIf
  If pin = 61 Or pin = 62
    ProcedureReturn "one of the two board-identity straps that firmware reads to tell which board it is running on, set by fixed dividers on the schematic"
  EndIf
  ;  Tier 0 too, ruled 2026-09-02: the eGPIO range. Pads 105..126 are the
  ;  TLMM's eGPIO lines, co-owned by another subsystem (function 9), and on
  ;  THIS board 107..126 have no copper at all - sheet 9 draws 105..112 as
  ;  no-connects and 113..126 are absent from the symbol. GPIO 109 and 110
  ;  were seen changing with nothing attached: the other owner driving a pad
  ;  that goes nowhere. None of the thirteen SoC-to-STM32 link pins (highest
  ;  is 80) is in the range, so reserving it costs the bridge nothing.
  If pin >= 107 And pin <= 126
    ProcedureReturn "an eGPIO line shared with another subsystem that has no copper on this board: 109 and 110 change on their own with nothing attached, because the other owner is driving a pad that goes nowhere"
  EndIf
  If pin = 94 Or pin = 95
    ProcedureReturn "part of the forced-USB-boot (emergency download) strap pair: driving it can drop the board into its firmware recovery mode instead of booting"
  EndIf
  If pin = 45
    ProcedureReturn "the only SoC-driven power enable on this board: it gates the regulator feeding the ANX7625 DisplayPort bridge, so driving it powers that part down"
  EndIf
  If pin = 44 Or pin = 90
    ProcedureReturn "a control line of the ANX7625 DisplayPort bridge (its reset and its hot-plug-detect), which fights the USB-C power-delivery state machine if it is driven"
  EndIf
  If pin = 89
    ProcedureReturn "a USB PHY control line"
  EndIf
  If pin >= 102 And pin <= 104
    ProcedureReturn "part of the I2S audio bus carrying sound to the DisplayPort bridge, which the datasheet lists as interface-dedicated and not available as general-purpose I/O"
  EndIf

  ; ---- TIER A: actively owned by an enabled node ----------------------

  ; The console. unoq-live-device-tree.dts:795-804 (serial@4a90000,
  ; "qcom,geni-debug-uart", status okay), :1710 (qup-uart4-default-state,
  ; pins gpio12/gpio13), :2882 (serial0 alias) and :3011
  ; (stdout-path = "serial0"). Three independent statements that this is
  ; the debug console, and it is the wire this transcript is on.
  If pin = 12 Or pin = 13
    ProcedureReturn "the serial console this session's transcript is travelling on: the QUP serial engine 4 UART is muxed onto GPIO 12 and 13, and driving either would cut the console mid-command with no way to see why"
  EndIf

  ; Bluetooth. serial@4a8c000 (SE3) is a WCN3988 HCI UART with flow
  ; control on 8..11 (dts:618-627, :1585), and its enable line is
  ; GPIO 87 (dts:638, enable-gpios).
  If pin >= 8 And pin <= 11
    ProcedureReturn "part of the Bluetooth controller's HCI UART: the WCN3988 is wired to QUP serial engine 3 on GPIO 8 to 11, with hardware flow control"
  EndIf
  If pin = 87
    ProcedureReturn "the Wi-Fi and Bluetooth module's control line: the device tree uses it as the radio's enable and the schematic names it as the antenna switch control, so driving it disturbs the radio either way"
  EndIf

  ; The second UART. serial@4a88000 (SE2), a four-wire port on 6, 7, 71
  ; and 80 (dts:809-818, :1417).
  If pin = 6 Or pin = 7 Or pin = 71 Or pin = 80
    ProcedureReturn "part of a second on-board UART: QUP serial engine 2 is muxed onto GPIO 6, 7, 71 and 80 as a four-wire port"
  EndIf

  ; The two I2C buses, both enabled. i2c@4a80000 on 0/1 (dts:504-517,
  ; :1506) and i2c@4a84000 on 4/5 (dts:693-706, :1679) - the latter is
  ; the bus carrying the display bridge and seven other devices, and is
  ; the bus Anvil's own `i2c` command drives.
  If pin = 0 Or pin = 1
    ProcedureReturn "the SDA and SCL of I2C bus 0: QUP serial engine 0 is an I2C master on GPIO 0 and 1, and Anvil's own i2c command drives it"
  EndIf
  If pin = 4 Or pin = 5
    ProcedureReturn "the SDA and SCL of I2C bus 1: QUP serial engine 1 is the board's populated I2C bus on GPIO 4 and 5, carrying the display bridge and seven other devices"
  EndIf

  ; SPI 5 and its handshake pin. spi@4a94000 on 14..17 (dts:540-552,
  ; :1695) with a spidev child whose extra line is GPIO 37 (dts:556-559,
  ; :1493). GPIO 37 is ALSO named mcu-boot0-state (dts:1726) - the same
  ; pin under two names, which is the strongest hint in the whole file
  ; that this SPI port is the STM32 link.
  If pin >= 14 And pin <= 17
    ProcedureReturn "part of the SPI bus on QUP serial engine 5, GPIO 14 to 17, which carries the board's SPI device"
  EndIf
  If pin = 37
    ProcedureReturn "claimed twice over: it is the SPI device's handshake line on serial engine 5, and it is also the pin the device tree names as the co-processor's BOOT0, so driving it may put the STM32U585 into its bootloader"
  EndIf

  ; The display bridge. encoder@58 is the ANX7625 DSI-to-DisplayPort
  ; bridge on i2c-1 (dts:710-719); its interrupt is GPIO 81
  ; (dts:715, interrupts-extended) and its cable-detect is GPIO 46
  ; (dts:712 with the state at :1602).
  If pin = 81
    ProcedureReturn "the interrupt line from the ANX7625 DisplayPort bridge, the part that drives the USB-C video output"
  EndIf
  If pin = 46
    ProcedureReturn "the cable-detect input of the ANX7625 DisplayPort bridge, which tells it whether anything is plugged into the USB-C port"
  EndIf

  ; The LEDs. dts:2474-2520, each a gpios= property on a leds child.
  ; Cosmetic to drive, but they are owned, and one of them is the panic
  ; indicator - a light that lies is worse than no light.
  If pin = 39
    ProcedureReturn "the red panic LED, driven by the operating system as its panic indicator"
  EndIf
  If pin = 40
    ProcedureReturn "the green Wi-Fi activity LED"
  EndIf
  If pin = 41 Or pin = 42 Or pin = 60
    ProcedureReturn "one of the three user LEDs (red, green and blue) the operating system owns"
  EndIf
  If pin = 47
    ProcedureReturn "the blue Bluetooth LED, tied to the radio's power state"
  EndIf

  ; The two buttons, both wakeup sources. dts:2838 and :2847.
  If pin = 36 Or pin = 96
    ProcedureReturn "one of the two volume buttons, which are wakeup sources for the operating system"
  EndIf

  ; ---- TIER B: the STM32U585 bridge, by name only ---------------------
  ;
  ; Five orphaned pinctrl states name these pins for the co-processor -
  ; mcu-swdio (dts:1500), mcu-swclk (:1651), mcu-nrst (:1703),
  ; mcu-spi-rdy (:1687) - plus two more of unclear purpose,
  ; jmisc-gpio28 (:1579) and qup_spi0_jmisc (:1563, which adds GPIO 82
  ; and 86). NOTHING in the device tree references any of them, because
  ; the STM32 is not described in the device tree at all. The names are
  ; the only evidence there is, and they are enough: a pin called
  ; mcu_nrst resets the co-processor if it is driven.
  If pin = 25 Or pin = 26
    ProcedureReturn "part of the co-processor's SWD debug port (the device tree names GPIO 25 as its data line and 26 as its clock), so driving it interferes with debugging the STM32U585"
  EndIf
  If pin = 38
    ProcedureReturn "the co-processor's reset line by name in the device tree, so driving it would reset the STM32U585 and whatever it is running"
  EndIf
  If pin = 70
    ProcedureReturn "named as the co-processor's SPI ready handshake, an input the STM32U585 drives to say it has data"
  EndIf
  If pin = 28 Or pin = 82 Or pin = 86
    ProcedureReturn "named by a board-specific pin group in the device tree whose purpose is not stated there, so it is refused rather than driven blind"
  EndIf

  ; ---- TIER C: advisory - partners of live buses, disabled blocks -----
  ;
  ; Free by the letter of the device tree, but they are the alternate
  ; pins of controllers that are live on their other pins, or belong to
  ; a controller that is merely disabled rather than absent. Traces
  ; almost certainly exist.
  If pin = 2 Or pin = 3
    ProcedureReturn "an alternate-function pin of the serial engine that is currently running I2C bus 0 on its other two pins, so the board very likely has it routed"
  EndIf
  If pin = 22 Or pin = 23 Or pin = 29 Or pin = 30
    ProcedureReturn "a camera-control bus pin: the controller is disabled in the device tree rather than absent, so the pins are routed and something may still be attached"
  EndIf
  If pin = 69
    ProcedureReturn "the alternate SPI pin of the serial engine that is currently running I2C bus 1, so the board very likely has it routed"
  EndIf
  If pin = 97
    ProcedureReturn "the alternate SPI pin of the console's serial engine, whose partner pin is already in use as a volume button"
  EndIf

  ; Everything else is free to drive.
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
